WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.791
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.091 --> 00:00:09.443
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:16.610
Unit eight: Space Policy. Today you will state purpose and prevent danger with in order that / lest / so as not to.

4
00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.632
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:24.171
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:26.738
Space exploration.

7
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:33.000
Space exploration means the scientific study of space using crewed and robotic missions.

8
00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:37.056
Space exploration inspired a generation of scientists.

9
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.231
Your turn. Say: Space exploration.

10
00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:45.764
Space exploration.

11
00:00:46.517 --> 00:00:48.272
Satellite technology.

12
00:00:49.283 --> 00:00:54.000
Satellite technology means machines in orbit that send signals and images to Earth.

13
00:00:54.583 --> 00:00:57.465
Satellite technology guides ships and aircraft.

14
00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:01.840
Your turn. Say: Satellite technology.

15
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:06.479
Satellite technology.

16
00:01:07.217 --> 00:01:08.531
Orbital debris.

17
00:01:09.517 --> 00:01:14.094
Orbital debris means broken pieces of old spacecraft that circle the Earth.

18
00:01:14.683 --> 00:01:17.194
Orbital debris threatens working satellites.

19
00:01:17.783 --> 00:01:20.691
Your turn. Say: Orbital debris.

20
00:01:23.533 --> 00:01:24.883
Orbital debris.

21
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:26.745
Space race.

22
00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:32.362
Space race means the rivalry between countries to achieve firsts in space.

23
00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.379
The space race began during the Cold War.

24
00:01:35.983 --> 00:01:38.798
Your turn. Say: Space race.

25
00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:42.774
Space race.

26
00:01:43.517 --> 00:01:45.354
International space law.

27
00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.741
International space law means the rules that govern how countries may use space.

28
00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:54.255
International space law forbids claiming the Moon.

29
00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:58.408
Your turn. Say: International space law.

30
00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:03.173
International space law.

31
00:02:03.917 --> 00:02:05.475
Space agency.

32
00:02:06.483 --> 00:02:10.062
Space agency means a government body that runs space programmes.

33
00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.462
Each space agency shared its data openly.

34
00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.575
Your turn. Say: Space agency.

35
00:02:19.433 --> 00:02:20.841
Space agency.

36
00:02:21.583 --> 00:02:23.014
Commercial spaceflight.

37
00:02:24.017 --> 00:02:28.478
Commercial spaceflight means space travel and launches run by private companies.

38
00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:31.861
Commercial spaceflight has lowered launch costs.

39
00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.834
Your turn. Say: Commercial spaceflight.

40
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.409
Commercial spaceflight.

41
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:42.755
Space militarization.

42
00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:47.967
Space militarization means the use of space for military purposes.

43
00:02:48.583 --> 00:02:51.430
Space militarization worries many diplomats.

44
00:02:52.017 --> 00:02:54.843
Your turn. Say: Space militarization.

45
00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.653
Space militarization.

46
00:03:00.467 --> 00:03:04.791
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:11.344
Visal, a policy paper says 'Rules are needed in order that space remain safe.' What does this show?

48
00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:22.895
It shows purpose. 'In order that' + clause often takes the present subjunctive or should: in order that space remain safe. 'So that' is more common in speech.

49
00:03:23.983 --> 00:03:25.472
And what about 'lest'?

50
00:03:26.583 --> 00:03:38.143
'Lest' means 'in case something bad happens'. It is formal: 'Agencies track debris lest satellites collide.' It takes a base verb or should, with no negative.

51
00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:41.122
What about so as not to?

52
00:03:42.217 --> 00:03:53.646
Use 'so as not to' + verb for a negative purpose with the same subject: 'Agencies share data so as not to cause collisions.' Do not write 'lest not'.

53
00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.265
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:57.117 --> 00:04:01.705
Grammar focus: Purpose: in order that and lest and so as not to.

55
00:04:02.005 --> 00:04:03.169
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:03.883 --> 00:04:07.114
Rules are needed in order that space remain safe for everyone.

57
00:04:08.017 --> 00:04:11.491
Agencies track debris lest satellites collide in orbit.

58
00:04:12.383 --> 00:04:16.449
Agencies share data so as not to cause dangerous collisions.

59
00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:25.242
Express purpose with in order that + clause, lest + base verb (a feared danger) or so as not to + verb. Never write lest not.

60
00:04:25.983 --> 00:04:29.890
Be careful! Don't say: He hurried lest not he be late.

61
00:04:31.833 --> 00:04:34.437
Say: He hurried lest he be late.

62
00:04:34.737 --> 00:04:36.632
Do not add a negative after lest.

63
00:04:37.433 --> 00:04:39.993
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.695
Rules for the Final Frontier.

65
00:04:42.995 --> 00:04:49.546
Space exploration began as a race between rivals, but satellite technology now serves billions of ordinary people.

66
00:04:50.533 --> 00:04:54.832
Agencies track orbital debris lest a collision destroy a vital satellite.

67
00:04:55.833 --> 00:05:01.374
International space law exists in order that no single nation claim the Moon or other worlds.

68
00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:08.186
Commercial spaceflight is growing fast, so rules must be updated so as not to leave dangerous gaps.

69
00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:15.042
Many experts also warn against space militarization, lest a new race lead to conflict.

70
00:05:16.133 --> 00:05:20.170
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:21.017 --> 00:05:24.050
Agencies track debris blank satellites collide.

72
00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:27.759
A, lest. B, unless. C, whereas.

73
00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:33.776
The answer is A: lest.

74
00:05:34.917 --> 00:05:38.286
Pieces of broken equipment circling Earth are orbital blank.

75
00:05:38.586 --> 00:05:41.984
A, debris. B, law. C, tourism.

76
00:05:45.533 --> 00:05:47.266
The answer is A: debris.

77
00:05:48.417 --> 00:05:50.265
Choose the correct sentence.

78
00:05:50.565 --> 00:05:58.886
A, He left early lest not he miss the train.. B, He left early lest he miss the train.. C, He left early lest he misses the train..

79
00:06:02.433 --> 00:06:05.861
The answer is B: He left early lest he miss the train..

80
00:06:07.017 --> 00:06:10.630
A government body that runs space programmes is a space blank.

81
00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:14.258
A, bloc. B, agency. C, treaty.

82
00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.229
The answer is B: agency.

83
00:06:21.383 --> 00:06:24.648
According to the reading, satellite technology now serves…

84
00:06:24.948 --> 00:06:29.809
A, only soldiers. B, billions of ordinary people. C, no one yet.

85
00:06:33.367 --> 00:06:36.690
The answer is B: billions of ordinary people.

86
00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:41.098
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:45.842
Should there be stronger international laws governing activity in space?

88
00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:56.162
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:56.462 --> 00:07:22.560
I think there should be stronger international laws, because more countries and private companies now launch satellites. Orbital debris is increasing, and a single collision could disrupt communication for millions. Clear rules on traffic, debris and military use would protect everyone's access to space and prevent conflict. However, the rules must stay flexible enough to allow innovation.

90
00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.581
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:25.483 --> 00:07:31.445
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and purpose: in order that and lest and so as not to.

92
00:07:31.745 --> 00:07:36.452
Today's value is scientific curiosity. Ask why, and then go and find out.

93
00:07:36.752 --> 00:07:39.172
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:39.472 --> 00:07:42.740
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

